,"ingredient/maize"],["Mungbean","ingredient/mungbean"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],["Tylorrhynchus heterochetus","ingredient/tylorrhynchus-heterochetus"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice vermicelli","ingredient/rice-vermicelli"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Coconut cream","ingredient/coconut-cream"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Channidae","ingredient/channidae"],["Snakehead (fish)","ingredient/snakehead-fish"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"More from Vietnam","count":2,"links":[["Cham cuisine","cuisine/cham-cuisine"],["Vietnamese Chinese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/goan-catholic-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/goan-catholic-cuisine","n":"Goan Catholic cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Goan cuisine","cuisine/goan-cuisine"],["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Goan Catholic Cuisine is the cuisine of the Goan Catholic community and is largely influenced by  Portuguese cuisine. Due to over 450 years of Portuguese rule, the cuisine of Goan Catholics is dominated by ingredients and techniques of Portuguese cuisine like deep-frying, oven-baking, pork, vinegar, egg-based desserts, alcohol, etc."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"goan-catholic-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goan_Catholic_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Goan cuisine","cuisine/goan-cuisine"],["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Essential Goa Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/the-essential-goa-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Maria Teresa Menezes","note":"Penguin India collection strong on Goan Catholic dishes such as xacuti, sorpotel and bebinca.","url":"https://www.penguin.co.in/book/essential-goa-cookbook/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Goan cuisine","cuisine/goan-cuisine"],["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/7/7f/Goan_Xit_kodi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","w":500,"h":333,"by":"abcdz2000","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goan_Xit_kodi.jpg"}},"cuisine/tagalog-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tagalog-cuisine","n":"Tagalog cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Philippines","intro":[["The Tagalog people are an Austronesian ethnic group native to the Philippines, particularly the Metro Manila and Calabarzon regions and Marinduque province of southern Luzon, and comprise the majority in the provinces of Bulacan, Bataan, Nueva Ecija, Aurora, and Zambales in Central Luzon and the island of Mindoro."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tagalog-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tagalog_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/tohono-o-odham-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/tohono-o-odham-foodways","n":"Tohono O'odham foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Sonoran Desert, southern Arizona","names":"Tohono O'odham, O'odham","intro":[["The Tohono O'odham, the Desert People, live in the Sonoran Desert of southern Arizona. Their food comes from farming, gathering and hunting: ",["tepary beans","ingredient/phaseolus-acutifolius"],", squash, melons and chiles grown in fields watered by summer monsoon floods, and wild foods like cholla buds, prickly pear pads, mesquite beans and ",["acorns","ingredient/acorn"],"."],["In midsummer, before the rains, families gather ",["Saguaro fruit","ingredient/saguaro"]," with long poles made from saguaro ribs. The harvest marks the start of the O'odham new year, and the pulp becomes syrup, jam and wine. Tohono O'odham Community Action works to bring back traditional farming and foods, which are high in fiber and help keep blood sugar steady."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Cultural Survival Quarterly, Enjoying a low-fat desert","url":"https://www.culturalsurvival.org/node/3052"},{"name":"Tucson Spotlight and O'odham Media, Saguaro fruit harvest sustains Tohono O'odham traditions","url":"https://www.tucsonspotlight.org/saguaro-fruit-harvest-sustains-tohono-oodham-traditions/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"tohono-o-odham-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"dish/bakpia":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bakpia","n":"Bakpia","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Hopia, pia or bakpia (Javanese: ꦧꦏ꧀ꦥꦶꦪ, romanized: bakpia; Chinese: 肉餅; Pe̍h-ōe-jī: bah-piáⁿ; lit. 'meat pastry'- the name it is known by in Indonesia)  is a popular Indonesian and Philippine bean-filled moon cake-like pastry originally introduced by Fujianese immigrants in the urban centers of both nations around the past centuries. It is a widely available inexpensive treat and a favoured gift for families, friends and relatives."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bakpia"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bakpia"}]